oB5=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oCd="src/web/exercises/Tutorial.re.stitched",oCj=GB,oCk=tQ,oCv=djW,oCw=djW,oCx=[0,"Check the expression in the editor!",0],oCz=[0,cm],oCB=[0,0,0],oCC=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oCD=[0,iu,0],oCF=[0,E],oCH=[0,0,0],oCI=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oCJ=[0,cYg,0],oCL=[0,E],oCN=[0,0,0],oCO=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,7],5],[0,[0,7],5]]],oCP=[0,nP,0],oCR=[0,E],oCT=[0,0,0],oCU=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oCV=[0,Tn,0],oCX=[0,E],oCZ=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oC0=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oC1=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oC2=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oC4=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oC6=m,oC7="Arithmetic expressions are built compositionally by combining smaller expressions with operators like addition (`+`) and multiplication (`*`). The smallest arithmetic expressions are number literals, such as `2` and `42`.\n\nFor example, enter the expression `2 * 3 + 4 * 5` in the editor below and observe its computed value: `26`. This follows the standard order of operations, where multiplication precedes addition.\n\nTry moving your cursor through the program to see how Hazel visually groups operands according to their operators, making the structure of the expression clear.\n",oC8="Tu_ComposingArithmetic",oC9="Composing Arithmetic Expressions",oC_="f6a7b8c9-d012-3456-789a-bcdef0123456",oDa=[0,"Make sure you use x as your variable",0],oDc=[0,cm],oDe=[0,0,0],oDf=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oDg=[0,iu,0],oDi=[0,E],oDk=[0,0,0],oDl=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oDm=[0,"15",0],oDo=[0,E],oDq=[0,0,0],oDr=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,7],5],[0,[0,7],5]]],oDs=[0,nP,0],oDu=[0,E],oDw=[0,0,0],oDx=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oDy=[0,Tn,0],oDA=[0,E],oDC=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oDD=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oDE=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oDF=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oDH=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oDJ="Add a `+ 10` to your answer from the previous exercise.",oDK="The scope of a variable is the expression(s) of the program where it is available for use. For let expressions, the sub-expression that follows the `in` keyword has the variable bound by the let expression in scope. Hazel's expression decorations show you where the scope of the variable will end. \n 1. Define a variable x inside a let expression.\n 2. Assign it the value 5. \n 3. Use x in an arithmetic operation inside the in block to return x + 10",oDL="Tu_Scope",oDM="Scope",oDN="a7b8c9d0-1234-5678-9abc-def012345678",oDP=[0,"Did you type out the given expression correctly?",0],oDR=[0,cm],oDT=[0,0,0],oDU=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oDV=[0,iu,0],oDX=[0,E],oDZ=[0,0,0],oD0=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oD1=[0,cYg,0],oD3=[0,E],oD5=[0,0,0],oD6=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,7],5],[0,[0,7],5]]],oD7=[0,nP,0],oD9=[0,E],oD$=[0,0,0],oEa=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oEb=[0,Tn,0],oEd=[0,E],oEf=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oEg=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oEh=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oEi=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oEk=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oEm=c$s,oEn="To prove that `2 * 3 + 4 * 5 \xe2\x89\xa1 26` in grade school, we would have written out a series of equational steps, each simplifying the expression from the previous step by performing one elementary arithmetic computation at a time.\n\nUse Hazel's stepper by clicking the button to the right of the result below and interactively prove that our evaluation to `26` is correct one elementary arithmetic step at a time. This proof is the essence of computation!",oEo="Tu_ComputingEquationally",oEp="Computing Equationally",oEq="b8c9d0e1-2345-6789-0abc-def123456789",oEs=[0,"Have you shadowed x to 7?",0],oEu=[0,cm],oEw=[0,0,0],oEx=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oEy=[0,iu,0],oEA=[0,E],oEC=[0,0,0],oED=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oEE=[0,aNv,0],oEG=[0,E],oEI=[0,0,0],oEJ=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,7],5],[0,[0,7],5]]],oEK=[0,nP,0],oEM=[0,E],oEO=[0,0,0],oEP=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oEQ=[0,b3,0],oES=[0,E],oEU=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oEV=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oEW=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oEX=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oEZ=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oE1=m,oE2="Once a variable is defined, it cannot be changed. There is no assignment operator in languages based on pure mathematical expressions, where variables are given meaning by substitution, like Hazel. However, it is possible to define a `new variable` that shares the name of a previously bound variable. However, this makes it impossible to refer to the previous binding within the scope of the new binding; we say that variable has been `shadowed`. For example, the expression `let y = 8 in let y = 0 in x` would evaluate to 0. \n\nNow, let's try it. First, define a variable x to any number you want. Then, shadow x to the value 7. Make sure you that in both let expression the variable has the same name. ",oE3="Tu_Shadowing",oE4="Shadowing",oE5="c9d0e1f2-3456-7890-1abc-def234567890",oE7=[0,"Reread the question!",0],oE9=[0,cm],oE$=[0,0,0],oFa=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oFb=[0,iu,0],oFd=[0,E],oFf=[0,0,0],oFg=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oFh=[0,qq,0],oFj=[0,E],oFl=[0,0,0],oFm=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,7],5],[0,[0,7],5]]],oFn=[0,nP,0],oFp=[0,E],oFr=[0,0,0],oFs=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oFt=[0,Tn,0],oFv=[0,E],oFx=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oFy=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oFz=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oFA=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oFC=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oFE="Type 2 + 2 in the cell below \xf0\x9f\x91\x87",oFF='You might not have realized it, but you wrote your first computer programs in grade school in the form of arithmetic expressions! \n\nFor example, enter the program `2 + 2` in the expression editor below. Hazel operates like a calculator, computing the value of your expression by equationally simplifying it (i.e. evaluating it), here to the integer value `4`. The symbol `\xe2\x89\xa1` is pronounced "is equivalent to".',oFG="Tu_ExpressiveProgramming",oFH="Expressive Programming",oFI="d0e1f2a3-4567-8901-2abc-def345678901",oFK=[0,"Have you declared exp1? Remember that it has to evaluate to true.",[0,"Have you declared exp2? Remember that it has to evaluate to true.",[0,"Have you declared exp3? Remember that it has to evaluate to false.",[0,"Have you declared exp4? Remember that it has to evaluate to false.",0]]]],oFM=[0,cm],oFO=[0,0,0],oFP=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oFQ=[0,iu,0],oFS=[0,E],oFU=[0,0,0],oFV=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oFW=[0,"exp4",0],oFY=[0,0,0],oFZ=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,[0,4],5]]],oF0=[0,TD,0],oF2=[0,E],oF4=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oF5=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oF6=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oF8=[0,cm],oF_=[0,0,0],oF$=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oGa=[0,iu,0],oGc=[0,E],oGe=[0,0,0],oGf=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oGg=[0,"exp3",0],oGi=[0,0,0],oGj=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,[0,4],5]]],oGk=[0,TD,0],oGm=[0,E],oGo=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oGp=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oGq=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oGs=[0,cm],oGu=[0,0,0],oGv=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oGw=[0,iu,0],oGy=[0,E],oGA=[0,0,0],oGB=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oGC=[0,"exp2",0],oGE=[0,E],oGG=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oGH=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oGI=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oGK=[0,cm],oGM=[0,0,0],oGN=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oGO=[0,iu,0],oGQ=[0,E],oGS=[0,0,0],oGT=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oGU=[0,"exp1",0],oGW=[0,E],oGY=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oGZ=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oG0=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oG1=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oG3=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oG5=m,oG6='So far, we have only seen expressions that evaluate to integers. However, Hazel supports many other types of values, like booleans. There are two boolean values, `true` and `false`. \n \n Hazel\'s type system ensures that expressions are used in ways that make sense. Each expression has a type, which predicts the type of its value. The expressions in the previous slides all had type `Int`, which is the type of integers, whereas the boolean values have type `Bool`. You can see the type of the expression your cursor is on in the cursor inspector at the bottom of the screen. The symbol `:`  is pronounced "has type". \n \n Boolean values can be constructed by using comparison operators like `<`, `==`, and `>` on integers. For example, `2 < 3` evaluates to `true`. Booleans can also be combined using logical and (conjunction), `&&`, and logical or (disjunction), `||`. \n \n Now declare 4 variables (exp1, exp2, exp3, exp4), and make exp1 and exp2 evaluate to true, and exp3 and exp4 evaluate to false. Feel free to combine different operators using conjunction or disjunction.',oG7="Tu_Booleans_and_Types",oG8="Booleans and Types",oG9="e1f2a3b4-5678-9012-3abc-def456789012",oG$=[0,"Have you followed the same format at the example for your expression?",0],oHb=[0,cm],oHd=[0,0,0],oHe=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oHf=[0,iu,0],oHh=[0,E],oHj=[0,0,0],oHk=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oHl=[0,"81",0],oHn=[0,E],oHp=[0,0,0],oHq=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,7],5],[0,[0,7],5]]],oHr=[0,nP,0],oHt=[0,E],oHv=[0,0,0],oHw=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oHx=[0,Tn,0],oHz=[0,E],oHB=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oHC=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oHD=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oHE=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oHG=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oHI=m,oHJ="Given a boolean expression, we can use it to choose between two expressions using a `conditional expression`.\n\nFor example, `if 2 < 3 then 4 * 4 else 5 * 5` evaluates to `16`.\n\nWrite a conditional expression that checks if the number `162 < 165`. If it is, return `162 / 2`, otherwise return `162 * 3 + 1`.",oHK="Tu_Conditional_Expressions",oHL="Conditional Expressions",oHM="f2a3b4c5-6789-0123-4abc-def567890123",oHO=[0,"Did you copy in the given expression correctly?",0],oHQ=[0,cm],oHS=[0,0,0],oHT=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oHU=[0,iu,0],oHW=[0,E],oHY=[0,0,0],oHZ=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oH0=[0,yt,0],oH2=[0,E],oH4=[0,0,0],oH5=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,7],5],[0,[0,7],5]]],oH6=[0,nP,0],oH8=[0,E],oH_=[0,0,0],oH$=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oIa=[0,Tn,0],oIc=[0,E],oIe=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oIf=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oIg=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oIh=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oIj=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oIl=c$s,oIm="Functions are expressions that take other expressions as inputs and produce other expressions as outputs. For example,  `fun x -> x + 1` is a function that takes an integer expression as input and produces an integer expression as output. Functions in Hazel do not themselves have names. Instead, you can use a `let` expression to name a function. \n You apply a function to an argument expression by using parentheses in the usual way. For example, `let f = fun x -> x + 1 in f(2)` evaluates to `3` . Go through this example in the stepper to see how substitution of the function for the variable standing for the function works.",oIn="Tu_Functions",oIo=c0r,oIp="a3b4c5d6-7890-1234-5abc-def678901234",oIr=[0,"Make sure the expression after the keyword in is correct.",0],oIt=[0,cm],oIv=[0,0,0],oIw=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oIx=[0,iu,0],oIz=[0,E],oIB=[0,0,0],oIC=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oID=[0,aNv,0],oIF=[0,E],oIH=[0,0,0],oII=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,7],5],[0,[0,7],5]]],oIJ=[0,nP,0],oIL=[0,E],oIN=[0,0,0],oIO=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oIP=[0,Tn,0],oIR=[0,E],oIT=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oIU=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oIV=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]],oIW=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oIY=[0,0,0,0,0,0],oI0="Make sure your expression starts with `let` and ends with `in`.",oI1="As it turns out, you can enrich arithmetic expressions to go from simple integer computations to general-purpose computations! Over the next several slides, we will see how this works.\n\nOur first step is to introduce a way to abbreviate expressions using variables. Type the expression `let x = 2 * 3 in x + 1` into the expression editor below, observing that it evaluates to `7`. You can insert a new line after the `in` keyword to make the program more idiomatic (i.e. readable).\n\nVariables are given computational meaning simply by substitution. Use the stepper to see how this works.",oI2="Tu_Variables",oI3="Variables",oI4="b4c5d6e7-8901-2345-6abc-def789012345",oI6=[0,"Make sure to embed a let expression as shown in the description!",0],oI8=[0,cm],oI_=[0,0,0],oI$=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,10],5],[0,[0,10],5]]],oJa=[0,iu,0],oJc=[0,E],oJe=[0,0,0],oJf=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oJg=[0,agK,0],oJi=[0,E],oJk=[0,0,0],oJl=[0,5,0,[0,[0,[0,7],5],[0,[0,7],5]]],oJm=[0,nP,0],oJo=[0,E],oJq=[0,0,0],oJr=[0,5,0,[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oJs=[0,Tn,0],oJu=[0,E],oJw=[0,0,[0,1,0]],oJx=[0,5,[0,5,0],[0,[0,0,5],[0,0,5]]],oJy=[0,hx,[0,dX,0]]